From ae1b614374e34b0174aedcda683a2eb9d3d5aa9a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dfattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 12:45:58 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] inline-3d(#103): detect and report a dead weave session
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Today a weave-only client is the one client in the system that never learns its
connection died. displayxr-service restarts are routine (runtime #1002 exits the
service for relaunch on device-lost), and the browser's response is nothing at
all: the failed submits are counted by 0070 and the tiles go mono, but no state
changes, nothing crosses to the browser process, and the log says only "batch
weave submit failed" — the same line a bad frame produces.

This patch adds detection and reporting. It adds NO recovery: no ladder, no
reconnect attempt, no timers, no session teardown. Those are 0077, which is
gated on the E0 experiment (can a post-LowerToken GPU process open the runtime
pipe at all?). 0076 ships without any runtime dependency and gives field signal
before recovery exists.

DETECTION (GPU process, displayxr_weave_client.cc). One classifier,
NoteWeaveCallResult(), on the four channels that talk to the runtime on the
weave session: the batch submit, the per-element submit, the v8 sticky
screen-flat wish and the phase snap. All four, because which one notices first
depends on the page: an animating page detects on its next batch submit
(~16 ms), while an IDLE page submits nothing and its only edges are a popup
opening (sticky wish) or the window being dragged (snap).

The verdict is a failure STREAK — three consecutive failed calls, any success
anywhere resetting it — because that is all today's runtime gives us. The
XR_DXR_weave entry points bypass OXR_CHECK_XRET and hand-roll
XR_ERROR_RUNTIME_FAILURE, so a broken pipe is byte-identical to any other
refusal and never sets sess->has_lost, while every other IPC-backed call
(xrEndFrame, xrSyncActions, xrLocateViews, xrPollEvent) maps
XRT_ERROR_IPC_FAILURE to XR_ERROR_INSTANCE_LOST. The classifier already reads
XR_ERROR_INSTANCE_LOST / XR_ERROR_SESSION_LOST as a SINGLE-CALL verdict, so
when the runtime-side fix lands (#103 RC-2, runtime PR #1094) there is no new
plumbing — this simply stops having to wait for three.

REPORTING. displayxr_weave.mojom gains WeaveSessionLost(reason, last_result,
fail_count) on the existing DisplayXRWeaver interface — fire-and-forget, since
there is nothing the browser could reply that the GPU could act on and a round
trip on the present sequence is the last thing a frame that is already losing
wants. It rides a remote sync mode already has open and idle (Bind() is
unconditional under --enable-inline-3d). The GPU side takes the client's
verdict take-and-clear in ReportWeaveSessionLostIfDetected(), from the submit
paths, i.e. the same GPU-main/present sequence WeaveSubmit is dispatched from —
so exactly one message crosses per outage.

The browser has to know because it owns the other half of the problem: its own
snap/query session backs LocateViewsForRect and therefore every inline-3D
SCENE, and it is the only process that can ever relaunch displayxr-service (a
sandboxed GPU process cannot spawn children). 0076 records and logs that; 0077
acts on it.

STATE + LOGGING (design §6.5, §9). One weave-mode state word replaces the
accumulating booleans: LIVE / DEGRADED / DISCONNECTED / RECONNECTING /
RECOVERED / SKEW. 0076 drives the first three (kDegraded/kLive mirror 0071's
existing transitions; the |degraded| bool stays authoritative for 0071's own
logic), and a DISCONNECT SUPERSEDES A DEGRADE — you cannot re-negotiate the rig
on a dead pipe. Both new lines are counted LOG(ERROR), first-3-then-1-in-300,
tagged [DisplayXR][weave-mode], and the GPU-side one keeps §9's prefix verbatim
so a grep written against the design matches:

  [DisplayXR][weave-mode] DISCONNECTED: weave session lost (result=<r>
  fails=<n> generation=<g>) — tiles render MONO until reconnect [channel=...]

|generation| is always 0 here (nothing reconnects yet) and is carried so the
line shape is final and 0077 only has to increment it.

NEGATIVE CONTROL (design §9, required). Both the classifier and the browser's
receiving end return early unless a weave session was ever established in this
process. On a machine with no DisplayXR runtime InitOutcome::kUnavailable
latches, the latch stays false, and none of this can fire — no log line, no
report, no counter. The browser's guard is the load-bearing one, because that
entry point is driven by an IPC message rather than by our own state.

Deliberately deferred: the sticky-wish and phase-snap channels are CLASSIFIED
and LOGGED the instant they fail, but their mojo push to the browser rides the
next GPU frame rather than firing from the display-mode receiver's sequence.
Reaching the weaver_ remote from there would be a cross-sequence mojo hazard
for no gain in 0076, where the browser has no ladder to arm.

Refs: DisplayXR/displayxr-browser#103, #99, #92, #96; runtime #1002, #1094
